.TH NIMDOW-WL 1 "" "nimdow-wl"
.SH NAME
nimdow-wl \- a dynamic tiling window manager for the River Wayland compositor
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B river \-c nimdow-wl
.br
.B nimdow-wl
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B nimdow-wl
.BI \-\-status " TEXT"
.br
.B nimdow-wl
.BR \-\-dumpState " [" \fIFILE\fR ]
.br
.B nimdow-wl
.BI \-\-COMMAND " " \fR[\fIARG\fR]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nimdow-wl
is a dynamic tiling window manager.
It is a window management client of the River Wayland compositor
(version 0.5 or later, using the river-window-management-v1 protocol):
River handles rendering and input devices, nimdow-wl decides how windows are laid out.
Windows are tiled automatically and organized with tags.
Layout and focus are controlled with keybindings from the configuration file,
or via IPC.
nimdow-wl draws its own status bar and provides a system tray
(StatusNotifierItem, with dbusmenu popups).
.
.PP
.B nimdow-wl
also acts as a cli client,
which talks to a running nimdow-wl process via a UNIX socket.
All window manager controls are available via the IPC and cli client.
The UNIX socket is created at
.B $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/nimdow-wl/ipc-socket.%p
where %p is the PID of nimdow-wl
(\fB/tmp\fR is used if XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is not set).
.
.PP
Floating windows are supported,
and can be moved/resized with the mouse while holding a modifier key,
or with the keyboard (super+ctrl+h/j/k/l moves, super+ctrl+shift+h/j/k/l resizes, super+c centers).
A tiled window made floating is centered at
.B floatingWidth
x
.B floatingHeight
percent (default 50 x 50) of the usable area the first time,
and gets its last floating geometry back afterwards.
The settings
.BR floatingWidth ", " floatingHeight
(10 to 100) and
.B floatingStep
(pixels, default 40) are in the
.B [settings]
table of the config file.
.
.PP
The
.B lockSession
control runs the
.B lockCommand
setting (default
.IR "swaylock -f -c 282828" ).
While the session is locked all key and pointer bindings are disabled, except the
.B [[startProcess]]
entries with
.BR "whileLocked = true" .
.
.PP
The
.B toggleHotkeyOverlay
control (Super+/) lists all key bindings of the loaded config, centred on the focused output.
Escape, a click or the key again closes it, as does locking the session.
A
.B [[startProcess]]
entry can have a
.B description
that is shown instead of its command.
With
.B hotkeyOverlayAtStartup = true
in
.B [settings]
the list opens when nimdow-wl starts.
.
.PP
The
.B [input.keyboard]
table sets the xkb keymap
.RB ( layout ", " variant ", " options ", " model ),
the key repeat
.RB ( repeatRate
in keys per second,
.B repeatDelay
in milliseconds) and
.BR numlock .
The
.B [input.touchpad]
table (libinput devices with tap support) takes
.BR tap ", " naturalScroll ", " disableWhileTyping ", " accelSpeed " (-1.0 to 1.0),"
.BR accelProfile " (adaptive, flat),"
.BR clickMethod " (clickfinger, buttonareas) and"
.BR scrollMethod " (twofinger, edge, none)."
The
.B [input.mouse]
table (the other libinput pointers) takes
.BR naturalScroll ", " accelSpeed ", " accelProfile " and " leftHanded .
Every key is optional: a key that is not set leaves the default alone.
Invalid values are logged and ignored.
The settings are applied at startup, when a device appears and when the config is reloaded,
and only to devices that support them.
A rejected keymap is logged and River keeps its own.
.
.SH STARTING
Select the
.B nimdow-wl
session in your login manager
(it runs
.BR "river -c nimdow-wl" ),
or start it from a virtual console with
.BR "river -c nimdow-wl" .
Running
.B nimdow-wl
outside of River does not work.
.
.PP
On startup nimdow-wl imports the display environment (WAYLAND_DISPLAY, XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP, ...)
into the systemd user manager and the D-Bus activation environment,
and starts
.BR nimdow-wl-session.target .
That target binds
.B graphical-session.target
and pulls in
.BR xdg-desktop-autostart.target ,
so XDG autostart entries are run.
The target is stopped again when nimdow-wl exits.
Set
.B NIMDOW_NO_SESSION=1
to skip all of this, for example when running nested or in tests.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.
.SS "Generic Commands"
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version \fR
Prints the version information.
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE" "\fR, \fB\-\-config=\fIFILE\fR"
Starts nimdow-wl with the given config file.
.
.SS "Special IPC Commands"
.TP
.BI \-\-status " TEXT"
Sets the text shown in the status bar
(e.g. from a script: \fBnimdow-wl \-\-status "$(date)"\fR).
See
.B BAR TEXT AND CLICK REGIONS
for colours and click actions.
.TP
.BR \-\-dumpState " [" \fIFILE\fR ]
Makes the running instance write its state (monitors, tags, windows, bar, menus) as JSON.
Meant for debugging, bug reports and tests.
.
.SS "IPC Commands"
Every window manager control of the
.B [controls]
table is also a command line option.
The dashes are optional and the case is ignored:
.B nimdow-wl \-\-toggle\-floating
is the same as
.BR "nimdow-wl togglefloating" .
Commands that take a tag number are used as
.BR "nimdow-wl gototag 3" .
.
.TP
.BR --reload-config
Reloads all settings from your config file.
.
.TP
.BR --increase-master-count
Increases the number of master windows in the layout.
.
.TP
.BR --decrease-master-count
Decreases the number of master windows in the layout.
.
.TP
.BR --move-window-to-previous-monitor
Moves the selected window to the previous monitor.
.
.TP
.BR --move-window-to-next-monitor
Moves the selected window to the next monitor.
.TP
.BR --focus-previous-monitor
Focuses the previous monitor (wraps around to the last monitor).
.
.TP
.BR --focus-next-monitor
Focuses the next monitor (wraps around to the first monitor).
.
.TP
\fB--go-to-tag\fR \fITAG\fR
Views the selected tag.
.
.TP
.BR --go-to-left-tag
Goes to the tag on the left.
.
.TP
.BR --go-to-right-tag
Goes to the tag on the right.
.
.TP
.BR --go-to-previous-tag
Views the previously viewed tag.
.
.TP
.BR --move-window-to-previous-tag
Moves the selected window to the previously viewed tag.
.
.TP
\fB--toggle-tag-view\fR \fITAG\fR
Toggles the visibility of a tag.
.
.TP
\fB--toggle-window-tag\fR \fITAG\fR
Toggles the assignment of a window to a tag.
.
.TP
.BR --focus-previous
Focuses the previous window.
.
.TP
.BR --focus-next
Focuses the next window.
.
.TP
.BR --move-window-previous
Moves the window to the previous position in the window stack.
.
.TP
.BR --move-window-next
Moves the window to the next position in the window stack.
.
.TP
\fB--move-window-to-tag\fR \fITAG\fR
Moves the selected window to the tag associated with the given number.
.
.TP
.BR --move-window-to-left-tag
Moves the window to the tag on the left.
.
.TP
.BR --move-window-to-right-tag
Moves the window to the tag on the right.
.
.TP
.BR --toggle-fullscreen
Toggles the fullscreen state of the selected window.
.
.TP
.BR --destroy-selected-window
Destroys the selected window.
.
.TP
.BR --toggle-floating
Toggles the floating state of the selected window.
.
.TP
.BR --move-floating-left ", " --move-floating-down ", " --move-floating-up ", " --move-floating-right
Moves the selected floating window by
.B floatingStep
pixels.
.
.TP
.BR --resize-floating-narrower ", " --resize-floating-taller ", " --resize-floating-shorter ", " --resize-floating-wider
Resizes the selected floating window by
.B floatingStep
pixels.
.
.TP
.BR --center-floating
Centers the selected floating window in the usable area.
.
.TP
.BR --increase-master-width
Increases the width of the master window.
.
.TP
.BR --decrease-master-width
Decreases the width of the master window.
.
.TP
.BR --rotate-clients
Rotate clients clockwise, making the last window master
.
.TP
.BR --move-window-to-scratchpad
Moves the selected window to the scratchpad.
.
.TP
.BR --pop-scratchpad
Brings back the window most recently added to the scratchpad.
.
.TP
.BR --toggle-statusbar
Hides or shows the status bar on the selected monitor.
.
.TP
.BR --lock-session
Runs the
.B lockCommand
setting.
.
.TP
.BR --toggle-hotkey-overlay
Shows or hides the key bindings overlay.
.
.TP
.BR --exit-session " (also " --exitSession )
Exits nimdow-wl (River exits with it).
.
.SH BAR TEXT AND CLICK REGIONS
The status text may contain ANSI SGR colour sequences
.RB ( ESC [ ... m ):
0 resets, 30\-37 and 90\-97 set the foreground, 40\-47 and 100\-107 the background,
39 and 49 restore the defaults,
38;5;N and 48;5;N select a 256 colour palette entry,
38;2;R;G;B and 48;2;R;G;B a 24 bit colour,
and 11\-20 select the 1st to 10th font of
.BR barFonts .
Other escape sequences are dropped, and line breaks and tabs become spaces.
.
.PP
The byte 0x1F (unit separator) splits the text into segments.
Clicking the bar runs the
.B [[startProcess]]
entry whose
.B clickRegion
is the number of the clicked part:
0 is the window title and 1, 2, ... are the segments of the status text, left to right.
Tags are not regions (a click switches to the tag).
An entry with
.B clickRegion
does not use
.BR keys .
In the command these placeholders are replaced:
.TP
.B %1
Index of the clicked character in the region, counted from 0.
.TP
.B %2 %3
X and Y position of the region.
.TP
.B %4 %5
X and Y position of the click.
.TP
.B %6
Width of the region.
.TP
.B %7
Button: 1 left, 2 middle, 3 right, 4 scroll up, 5 scroll down.
.TP
.B %0
All of %1 to %7, separated by spaces.
.PP
Example, two clickable regions (1 and 2):
.PP
.RS
.nf
\fBnimdow-wl \-\-status "$(printf 'one\e037two')"\fR
.fi
.RE
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration is a TOML file, see
.B FILES
for where it is found.
.B /usr/share/nimdow-wl/config.default.toml
documents every key.
The file is reloaded with the
.B reloadConfig
control (Super+Shift+R).
Its tables are:
.TP
.B [settings]
Window, bar, tray, layout, floating, scratchpad and general settings
(colours, fonts, gaps,
.BR lockCommand ", " modKey ", ...)."
.TP
.B [controls]
Key bindings for the window manager controls (the IPC commands),
as
.B [controls.NAME]
tables with
.B keys
and
.BR modifiers .
Modifiers: control, shift, alt (mod1), super (mod4), mod3, mod5.
.TP
.B [[startProcess]]
Runs a command.
Keys:
.BR command ,
.B keys
and
.BR modifiers ,
.B description
(shown in the key bindings overlay),
.B whileLocked
(stay active while the session is locked) and
.B clickRegion
(see above).
.TP
.B [[appRule]]
Window rules. The first rule whose
.BR class ", " instance " and " title
all match applies (both
.B class
and
.B instance
are compared with the app_id).
Matching is exact and case-sensitive;
a leading and/or trailing * matches a suffix, prefix or substring,
and an empty field matches everything.
Effects:
.B monitor
(1 is the first),
.B tags
and
.BR state " (normal, floating, fullscreen)."
For floating windows
.BR x ", " y ", " width " and " height
(pixels, relative to the output) set position and size.
.TP
.B [autostart]
.B exec
is a list of shell commands run at startup.
.TP
.B [monitors]
Tag names and the master window count and width per monitor and tag.
.TP
.B [input.keyboard], [input.touchpad], [input.mouse]
Input device settings, see above.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nimdow-wl/config.toml
The configuration file (\fB~/.config/nimdow-wl/config.toml\fR by default).
If it does not exist, the first
.B nimdow-wl/config.toml
in
.B $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
(default
.BR /etc/xdg ,
installed by the nimdow-wl-desktop-git package) or else the built-in default is
copied there at startup, and then used. An existing file is never overwritten.
.TP
.B /etc/xdg/nimdow-wl/config.toml
The system-wide default configuration of the desktop package.
.TP
.B /usr/share/nimdow-wl/config.default.toml
Reference copy of the built-in default configuration.
.TP
.B $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/nimdow-wl/ipc-socket.PID
The IPC socket.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/systemd/user/nimdow-wl-session.target
The systemd user target started with the session.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NIMDOW_NO_SESSION
If set to a non-empty value, nimdow-wl does not touch the systemd user session
(no environment import, no
.BR nimdow-wl-session.target ).
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Directory of the IPC socket.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory of the user configuration.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR river (1),
.BR systemd.special (7)
